{
  "id": "tonadilla_escenica",
  "name": "Tonadilla escénica (Madrid theatre song-interlude)",
  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "The tonadilla escénica was a short sung comic interlude performed between the acts of plays in Madrid's public theatres, the Príncipe and the Cruz, from about the 1750s into the early nineteenth century, built from songs, duets and closing seguidillas for one or several singer-actors with a small theatre orchestra of strings with oboes or flutes and horns. Luis Misón, Pablo Esteve and Blas de Laserna were among its principal composers, many scores survive in Madrid's Biblioteca Histórica Municipal, and early-music performers have revived the repertoire on modern recordings.",
  "recipe": "classic European mid-1750s tonadilla escénica, art Music, western common practice branch. one note per syllable, solo with instrumental accompaniment. norway spruce European alpine European maple bosnian source flamed figure violin orchestral, hand stopped natural horn, norway spruce European alpine European maple bosnian source flamed figure cello. shoebox concert hall. blumlein stereo pair, ultra-clean solid-state pre. tape 15ips hall reverb.",
